course: biology
lesson: 164
title: Madagascar: chameleons, leaf-tailed geckos, tenrecs, baobabs and a moth Darwin predicted
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Madagascar is a laboratory of the strange: about half of all the world's chameleon kinds, a gecko that is a dead leaf with eyes, a beetle with a neck like a crane, and trees that look planted upside down.
explain: A chameleon's tongue shoots out faster than you can blink, longer than its own body, and snaps back with a cricket stuck to its sticky tip, like a party blower that grabs a sweet.
explain: Cartoons show chameleons turning tartan on a tartan blanket; a real chameleon changes colour mostly to show its mood and signal to others, and to warm up or cool down.
explain: In 1862 Charles Darwin looked at an orchid whose nectar sat at the bottom of a tube about thirty centimetres long and said a moth with a tongue that long must exist; it was found after his death, and it was named praedicta, meaning predicted.
fact: A chameleon's two eyes can swivel separately, and chameleons shoot out their tongues in a fraction of a second, faster than a person can blink, and Madagascar has about half of all the chameleon kinds in the world.
fact: Chameleons change colour mostly to show their feelings and signal to other chameleons, and to warm up or cool down; they cannot copy any pattern they sit on.
fact: Brookesia nana, described in 2021, is one of the smallest reptiles known, small enough to sit on a fingertip, and was found by scientists searching leaves with torches at night.
fact: The satanic leaf-tailed gecko has a body and tail that look like a dry curled leaf with nibbled edges; it has no eyelids and licks its eyes clean.
fact: The male giraffe weevil has a neck several times longer than its body, and it is found only in Madagascar.
fact: Streaked tenrecs rub special quills together to make a sound to talk to each other.
fact: Tenrecs look like hedgehogs or shrews but are actually related to elephants and aardvarks, African animals that dig for ants.
fact: Baobabs have huge fat trunks that store water and short branches that look like roots; six of the eight kinds of baobab grow only in Madagascar.
fact: The tomato frog is bright red to warn animals, and when a snake grabs it, it swells up and oozes sticky glue-like slime.
fact: Madagascar hissing cockroaches hiss by pushing air out of breathing holes on their sides, not by rubbing body parts as most noisy insects do; like all insects, they have six legs.
fact: The Madagascar periwinkle contains chemicals used to make medicines for some childhood cancers, which shows why losing rare plants may mean losing future medicines.
fact: Darwin predicted a moth with a tongue about thirty centimetres long from the shape of the star orchid; the moth was found in 1903, after his death, and named with the word praedicta, meaning predicted.
exercise: How fast does a chameleon shoot out its tongue? | In a fraction of a second, faster than a blink | In about a minute | In about ten seconds | It never shoots its tongue out, because the tongue is stuck to its jaw
exercise: How many of the world's chameleon kinds live in Madagascar? | About half | Only one or two | About one in a hundred | All of them
exercise: Why do chameleons mostly change colour? | To show feelings, signal to others and control warmth | To copy any pattern they happen to sit on, even a chessboard | Because they are ill | To become invisible to people
exercise: What is Brookesia nana? | One of the smallest reptiles known | The biggest chameleon in the world | A kind of tiny frog | A flower that eats ants
exercise: How did scientists find tiny chameleons like Brookesia nana? | Searching leaves with torches at night | Looking down from planes | Catching them in big fishing nets dragged through the trees | Waiting at the zoo
exercise: What does the satanic leaf-tailed gecko look like? | A dry curled leaf with nibbled edges | A bright red flower | A shiny green stone | A long black stick with yellow spots on it
exercise: How does a leaf-tailed gecko clean its eyes? | It licks them, because it has no eyelids | It blinks very fast | It washes them in the rain and wipes them on the leaves | Its mother cleans them
exercise: What is special about the giraffe weevil? | The male has a neck several times longer than its body | It can run as fast as a giraffe on its long thin legs | It has spots like a giraffe | It eats giraffe hair
exercise: How do streaked tenrecs talk to each other? | They rub special quills together to make a sound | They flash their eyes | They thump their long tails hard on the ground like a drum | They whistle through their noses
exercise: Which animals are tenrecs related to? | Elephants and aardvarks | Hedgehogs and porcupines | Mice, rats and other rodents | Cats, dogs and other meat-eaters
exercise: Why does a baobab look upside down? | Its short branches look like roots in the air | It really grows with its roots on top, because it was planted upside down | It is always planted upside down | Elephants push it over every year
exercise: How many of the eight kinds of baobab grow only in Madagascar? | Six | Only one | Eight | None
exercise: What does a tomato frog do when a snake grabs it? | Swells up and oozes sticky slime | Plays dead and turns white all over, so the snake thinks it is ill | Spits venom in its eye | Screams until the snake lets go
exercise: Why is the tomato frog bright red? | To warn animals not to bite it | To hide among the ripe red tomatoes | To keep warm in the hot sun all through the day | To attract the flies it likes to eat
exercise: How does a Madagascar hissing cockroach hiss? | By pushing air out of breathing holes on its sides | By rubbing its legs together | By clicking its jaws | By flapping its stiff wings very fast against its body
exercise: Why does the Madagascar periwinkle matter to doctors? | Medicines for some childhood cancers are made from it | It cures every disease | It is used to stop bleeding from cuts and grazes straight away | It makes people sleep
exercise: How did Darwin know a moth with a long tongue must exist? | An orchid's nectar sat at the end of a tube about thirty centimetres long | He saw the moth flying around in his own garden one night | A sailor sent him a moth | He dreamed about it
exercise: What does the word praedicta in the moth's name mean? | Predicted | Long tongue | Night flyer | Darwin's friend
test: In a cartoon a chameleon on a chessboard turns into black and white squares. What is real? | Real chameleons change colour mostly for mood and warmth, not to copy patterns | They really copy any pattern | Chameleons cannot change colour at all; that only happens in cartoons | They only change colour in their sleep
test: «lol this hedgehog thing from madagascar, it's basically a hedgehog yeah» What is right? | No, tenrecs only look like hedgehogs; they are related to elephants | Yes, it is an ordinary hedgehog | Yes, it is a hedgehog that swam there from Africa a long time ago | No, it is a kind of cactus
test: «3 chameleons on the branch, how many eyes that can swivel» | 6 | 3 | 9 | 12
test: «a cockroach that hisses?? it's rubbing its legs innit» What is right? | No, it pushes air out of breathing holes on its sides | Yes, like a cricket | Yes, with its back legs | No, it hisses by rubbing its wings together, like a grasshopper
test: What would happen if the long-tongued moth died out? | The star orchid could lose the only animal that can reach its nectar and carry its pollen | The orchid would simply grow a much shorter tube the very next day, so other insects could reach the nectar | Other insects would fill the tube with nectar | Nothing, orchids need no animals
test: «my brother stepped on a dead leaf and it ran away screaming, lying or what» What could it have been? | A leaf-tailed gecko that looks like a dead leaf | A baobab seed | A tomato frog | A periwinkle flower
test: A snake bites a red frog, then drops it and wipes its mouth on the ground. What happened? | The tomato frog oozed sticky slime | The frog bit the snake back on the nose with its sharp teeth | The frog was only a plastic toy frog | The snake was simply not hungry that day
test: Why might losing a rare plant in Madagascar be bad for people far away? | It might hold a medicine no one has found yet | People need its wood for building houses and boats | It makes the air in Europe | All bread is made from it
test: «2 tenrecs n a hissing cockroach, how many legs» | 14 | 8 | 10 | 12
test: «darwin just guessed, lucky guess» What is right? | He reasoned from the orchid's long tube that such a moth must exist | He had already seen the moth | He made it up for a joke, and the moth was never found | He bred the moth himself
test: «baobabs grow upside down, my teacher said lol» What is right? | They only look upside down because the branches look like roots | Yes, roots grow on top | Yes, they really grow from the sky down to the ground | No, baobabs have no branches at all
exam: Identify the principal purposes for which the chameleon effects changes in its colouration. | The display of mood, communication with others and regulation of body temperature | The exact reproduction of any pattern upon which the animal happens to rest, even a printed page | The attraction of insect prey by means of flashing | Concealment from human observers exclusively
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the evolutionary relationships of the tenrec. | It is related to the elephants and the aardvarks | It is a true hedgehog of the family of hedgehogs | It is a species of rodent | It is a species of marsupial
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the means by which the Madagascar hissing cockroach produces its sound? | Air is expelled through breathing openings situated on its flanks | The hind legs are rubbed vigorously against the edges of the wings, as in the cricket | The jaws are struck rapidly together | The wings are vibrated at great speed
exam: Ascertain the basis upon which Charles Darwin predicted the existence of a moth with an exceptionally long tongue. | The depth of the nectar tube of a Madagascar orchid of about thirty centimetres | The discovery of such a moth in his own garden | A specimen dispatched to him by a sailor | The description of such a moth in an ancient text from the island, written by its first explorers
